Alembic migration environment for SmartSweep.

Layout
------
env.py            Reads DATABASE_URL from app.core.config; target_metadata comes
                  from app.db.base_models.
script.py.mako    Template for new revisions, with a review checklist in the
                  docstring.
versions/         The migration scripts themselves, named 0001_*, 0002_*, ...

Commands (run from Backend/)
----------------------------
  uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "0001 sprint 1 tables"
  uv run alembic upgrade head
  uv run alembic downgrade -1
  uv run alembic current
  uv run alembic history --verbose

Rules
-----
1. Autogenerate, then review by hand. Alembic is good at columns and tables and
   unreliable about type changes, renames, constraints, and anything involving
   data. A rename is autogenerated as drop + add, which silently deletes the
   column's data.
2. Never edit a migration that has been applied anywhere but your own machine.
   Roll forward with a new revision instead. Editing one leaves every other
   developer's alembic_version row pointing at a script that no longer exists.
3. Add every new model module to app/db/base_models.py before autogenerating.
   Alembic cannot see a model whose module was never imported, and will helpfully
   offer to drop the table it does not know about.
4. Write downgrade(). It is the only thing that makes a bad deploy reversible,
   and autogenerate usually gets it right for free.
5. Data migrations go in their own revision, separate from schema changes, so a
   failed backfill does not force a schema rollback.

Sprint 1 (migration 0001), ten tables:
  users, wards, complaints, complaint_status_history, workers, vehicles,
  equipment, tasks, task_workers, task_equipment
